## 4. The Myth of Perfect Attunement

There is a particular kind of pressure that modern parenting culture places on adults: the expectation that good caregiving requires continuous emotional accuracy.

Notice every signal.

Interpret it correctly.

Respond immediately.

Never become frustrated.

Never misread.

Never lose patience.

Never be unavailable.

This is not what healthy human interaction looks like.

Infants and caregivers are frequently out of sync.

One person turns away when the other seeks engagement. A parent misreads a tired cry as hunger. A baby becomes upset just as the caregiver is trying to finish another task. A soothing strategy works one evening and fails the next.

Relationships contain disruption.

The important developmental question is what happens next.

A mismatch may be followed by reconnection. A delayed response may be followed by comfort. A failed attempt at soothing may be replaced by another. An interaction can break and then resume.

This is the logic of repair.

Repair should not be romanticized into a universal law of development; the precise contribution of repair to later outcomes remains an area of active research. But the developmental idea is important because it offers a more realistic standard than perfection. The evidence does not require uninterrupted attunement, and the dossier explicitly identifies the minimum effective level of responsiveness and the role of repair as unresolved questions.

A child does not need every interaction to go well.

The child needs a relationship in which difficult interactions can, often enough, be restored.

That is a very different standard.

It leaves room for tired parents.

It leaves room for illness.

It leaves room for ordinary frustration.

It leaves room for human beings.

